<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jalayirid Sultanate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jalayirids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jalayirid Sultanate was a Mongol-descended dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in the fourteenth century. It emerged from the fragmentation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> after the death of Abu Sa'id in 1335. Its rise marks the dissolution of Mongol authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jalayirid-sultanate.jpg" alt="Jalayirid Sultanate" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Demis Map Server · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jalayirids,_Muzaffarids_in_1374.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Jalayirids came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalairs">Jalayir tribe</a>, a prominent Mongol tribe from the area of the River Onon in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. In the 14th century in West Asia they became largely Turkish-speaking, and they are credited with strengthening Turkic influence in Arabic-speaking Iraq. The dynasty was also called the Ilka dynasty or Ilkanids, after Ilka Noyan, great-grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-buzurg">Hasan Buzurg</a>. Ilge Noyan accompanied Hulegu to West Asia in the 1250s, took part in the siege of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a>' strongholds in Qohestan in 1256 and oversaw the rebuilding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> after its siege in 1258. After the death of Abu Sa'id in 1335, his family emerged as inheritors of the governors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>'s south-western lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-buzurg">Hasan Buzurg</a>, cousin of Abu Sa'id, installed Muhammad Khan on the throne at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and married Abu Sa'id's widow Dilshad Khatun. For a short time in 1337 and 1338 his authority was recognised across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, but expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> he held only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. He died in July 1356 and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-uways">Shaykh Uways</a> (1356 to 1374) took Azerbaijan after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> withdrew, and marks the peak of the dynasty. A weakening followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-jalayir">Sultan Ahmad Jalayir</a> executed his brother Shaikh Hussain after seizing Tabriz.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> attacked in 1384 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> in 1386. He captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1393, and Sultan Ahmad fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> and returned with their army. When Timur took Baghdad again in 1401 after a siege of forty days, the city was sacked and its people massacred. After Timur died in 1405, Ahmad recovered Baghdad with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf">Qara Yusuf</a> of the Qara Qoyunlu and held Tabriz from 1406 to 1410. Qara Yusuf defeated and killed him in 1410. The remnants were pushed into lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, around Hillah, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wasit">Wasit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, and the last ruler, Hosayn II, was pursued and killed after the Qara Qoyunlu prince Aspan captured Hillah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The administration was modelled on that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, with documents in Persian and Mongolian, and diplomatic correspondence in the Ilkhanid style with a red square seal bearing Arabic Islamic phrases. The Jalayirids were great patrons of Persianate culture, and their patronage marks an important period in the evolution of Persian art, where major aspects of later Persian painting developed. Their art was marked by increased naturalism, with parallels noted to contemporary Late Gothic trends and to Chinese art. Among illustrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-jalayir">Ahmad Jalayir</a> is known for the Khosrow and Shirin, made at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jalayirid Sultanate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Abu Saʿid died in 1335 without an heir, the Ilkhanate fragmented into rival successor states controlled by regional dynasties, including the <b>Jalayirids</b> and the Chupanids, who ruled through puppet khans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the fourteenth century they lived in the shadow of the <b>Jalayirid Sultanate</b> of Baghdad and Tabriz.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Jalayirids</b> presented themselves as heirs of the Ilkhans and patronised Persian culture: the poet Salman Savaji praised them, and Jalayirid manuscript painters, such as Junayd, shaped the later Timurid style.</blockquote><a href="/state/jalayirids">Jalayirids</a></li></ul></section>
